function Slide3() {
  return (
    <SlideShell>
      <SlideHeading title="Understanding Pronouns" />
      <ImagePlaceholder description="Illustrated pronoun options displayed on a clean, modern graphic" />

      <SectionLabel text="What are pronouns?" />
      <BodyText>
        Pronouns are the words we use to refer to someone when we are not using their name. In English: he, she, they, and others. For most of us, our pronouns feel obvious and assumed. For many people, particularly trans, non-binary, and gender non-conforming people, the pronouns others use for them are a significant part of how they are recognised or dismissed.
      </BodyText>

      <SectionLabel text="Common pronoun sets:" />
      <BulletList items={[
        "She/her: she went to the meeting; I spoke to her",
        "He/him: he is leading the project; I emailed him",
        "They/them: they are joining us next week; I forwarded it to them",
        "She/they or he/they: some people use more than one set and are comfortable with either",
        "Neopronouns such as xe/xem or ze/zir: less common but used by some people; treat them with the same respect",
      ]} />

      <SectionLabel text="Common misconceptions:" />
      <BulletList items={[
        "They/them for a single person is not grammatically incorrect: it has been used this way in English for centuries. We say 'someone left their umbrella' without a second thought.",
        "Using the right pronoun is not about agreeing with a political position: it is simply about referring to a colleague correctly",
        "You do not need to understand someone's gender identity to use their pronoun correctly: you just need to know what they have asked to be called",
      ]} />
    </SlideShell>
  );
}

