function Slide9() {
  return (
    <SlideShell>
      <SlideHeading title="Common Mistakes and How to Avoid Them" />
      <ImagePlaceholder description="A clean, minimal graphic representing learning and correction" />

      <SectionLabel text="Mistake 1: Only asking trans or non-binary people for their pronouns" />
      <BodyText>
        This singles people out and makes pronoun-sharing feel unusual or pointed. Instead, normalise the practice for everyone. When everyone shares pronouns, no one is singled out.
      </BodyText>

      <SectionLabel text="Mistake 2: Using preferred pronouns" />
      <BodyText>
        The word preferred implies that using someone's pronouns is optional, like a preference for tea over coffee. They are not a preference: they are the person's pronouns. Just say pronouns.
      </BodyText>

      <SectionLabel text="Mistake 3: Deadnaming" />
      <BodyText>
        A deadname is the name a trans person was given at birth and no longer uses. Using it, even accidentally, is harmful. If you learn someone's deadname, do not share it or use it. If you make this mistake, correct yourself briefly and move on.
      </BodyText>

      <SectionLabel text="Mistake 4: Asking intrusive questions" />
      <BodyText>
        Questions about someone's surgery, body, identity history, or birth name are not appropriate in a professional context. You do not need to understand someone's full gender history to use their pronouns correctly.
      </BodyText>

      <SectionLabel text="Mistake 5: Treating inclusive language as a one-off task" />
      <BodyText>
        Language habits are built over time. A single lesson is a starting point, not an endpoint. Regularly reflect on the language you use and look for opportunities to improve.
      </BodyText>
    </SlideShell>
  );
}

